# Code Style Guidelines
## CSS/SCSS
- **Color Format**: Use HSL/HSLA.
- **CSS Nesting & Comments**: Use native CSS nesting for cleaner, more maintainable code. Add concise comments above each selector (including nested ones) to help AI understand what HTML elements they target. Use the format: `/* Element description - <element class="class-name"> */` for simple selectors, or `/* Full CSS selector path - Element description */` for nested selectors. Examples: `/* Main tooltip container - <div class="info-tooltip"> */` or `/* .custom-button > .visual-style::after - Flash effect pseudo-element */`. **IMPORTANT**: Always verify comments against the associated Vue component template to ensure accuracy and completeness. **For property-specific comments, place them inline after the property** (e.g., `pointer-events: none; /* Don't block underlying controls */`) rather than on separate lines between properties.
- **Logical Properties**: Use logical CSS properties (e.g., `margin-inline`, `padding-block`).
- **Property Organization**: Alphabetize CSS properties within each rule. For CSS custom properties (variables), group related properties together (e.g., all `--bg-*` variables) but alphabetize within each group.
- **CSS Variables**: Place CSS variables at the top of a rule, followed by an empty line.
- **Individual Properties**: Use individual logical properties instead of shorthands. Simple shorthands like `margin: 0` or `padding: 1rem` are acceptable, but avoid complex shorthands like `margin: 0 5px 2px 3px` or `padding: 1rem 2rem 0.5rem`.
- **Component Styling**: Use scoped CSS files.
- **Global Styles**: Use global CSS files for shared styles.
- **CSS Specificity**: Prefer greater specificity over `!important`. Only use `!important` as a last resort when specificity cannot be increased.
- **Sub-component Styling**: When having difficulty styling sub-components, try using `:deep()` before resorting to other solutions.

## Vue.js Components
- Use Composition API with `<script setup>` syntax.

- Follow Vue 3 best practices.
- **Template Attributes**: Use kebab-case for all HTML attributes and component props in templates (e.g., `data-name`, `:keyboard-shortcut`, `:button-style-class`). This applies to both native HTML attributes and custom component props in the template section.
- **Props Usage**: Prefer `defineProps<...>()` directly over accessing the `props` object. Use `props` only when you need to destructure, pass to functions, or have complex prop logic that requires the full props object.
- Use self-closing tags for empty elements.
- Use Nuxt auto-imports; do not add component import statements.
- **Nuxt Icons**: The `<Icon>` component is auto-imported by Nuxt. Never add `import { Icon } from "#components"` or similar imports. Use Material Design Icons format: `<Icon name="mdi:icon-name" :size="20" />`.